*PORTLAND, Ore., October 29, 2026* — Northwind Instruments, Inc. today reported results for its third quarter of fiscal 2026, which ended September 27, 2026.

*Third quarter highlights*
- Revenue of \$681.5 million, up 8.1% year over year and flat sequentially.
- GAAP diluted earnings per share of \$1.42, compared with \$1.37 a year ago; non-GAAP diluted EPS of \$1.61, compared with \$1.55.
- Test & Measurement revenue reached a record \$412.6 million.
- Industrial Sensing revenue declined 4.5% sequentially to \$268.9 million.
- Free cash flow of \$88.0 million; \$50 million of shares repurchased during the quarter.

"Our Test & Measurement business delivered another record quarter, and demand from defense and aerospace laboratories has never been stronger," said Dana Whitfield, Chief Executive Officer. "Industrial Sensing had a difficult quarter. Two large automotive customers cut their build schedules, and we did not adjust our cost base quickly enough. We are taking decisive action, and I am confident that the consolidation of our sensor lines will restore segment margins to the mid-teens by the end of fiscal 2027."

"We are narrowing our full-year guidance to reflect a more cautious view of industrial demand," said Raj Menon, Chief Financial Officer. "Order intake in Test & Measurement grew 11% year over year and our backlog is the highest in company history, which gives us good visibility into the fourth quarter. At the same time, we expect wafer cost inflation and restructuring charges to weigh on gross margin for the next several quarters."

*Outlook*

For the fourth quarter of fiscal 2026, Northwind expects revenue of \$690 million to \$710 million and non-GAAP diluted EPS of \$1.58 to \$1.68. For the full fiscal year 2026, the company now expects revenue of \$2.71 billion to \$2.73 billion (previously \$2.70 billion to \$2.76 billion) and non-GAAP diluted EPS of \$6.20 to \$6.30 (previously \$6.20 to \$6.45). The company expects fiscal 2027 revenue growth in the mid-single digits, with Test & Measurement growing faster than Industrial Sensing, and expects restructuring charges of \$6 million to \$9 million over the next three quarters.

